APS-001 — Purpose & Scope

Document ID: APS-001
Title: Purpose & Scope
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the Atlas Policy Specification (APS) as the normative standard governing policy definition, evaluation, enforcement, lifecycle, and governance within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

APS transforms the architectural and data foundations established by ACC, AHS, and ADS into a formal, implementation-independent policy system capable of governing engineering cognition, workflows, data, security, and operational behavior.

2. Objectives

APS SHALL:

Define canonical policy concepts.
Define policy semantics.
Define policy evaluation.
Define policy enforcement.
Define policy governance.
Define policy lifecycle.
Define policy interoperability.
Define policy conformance.
3. Scope

APS governs policies related to:

Engineering cognition
Canonical Atlas Objects
Decision Records
Validation
Security
Trust
Lifecycle
Workflows
Communications
Events
Interoperability
Compliance
Governance

APS does not define:

Programming languages
Policy engines
Rule engines
Scripting languages
Infrastructure implementations

These belong to implementation profiles.

4. Relationship to Other Specifications

APS SHALL conform to:

ACC — Atlas Cognitive Codex
AHS — Atlas Hivemind Specification
ADS — Atlas Data Specification

Future Atlas specifications SHALL reference APS when defining behavioral or governance rules.

APS SHALL become the normative policy standard for Atlas.

5. Canonical Definition

A Policy is a formally governed statement that defines mandatory, permitted, prohibited, or conditional behavior for participants, objects, processes, or interactions within the Atlas ecosystem.

Policies govern behavior.

They do not directly perform behavior.

6. Policy Objectives

Every Atlas Policy SHALL aim to:

Preserve engineering integrity.
Preserve canonical semantics.
Protect engineering assets.
Enable trustworthy automation.
Support explainable governance.
Enable interoperability.
Enable regulatory compliance.
Support continuous evolution.
7. Design Principles

Every Atlas Policy SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit

Policies SHALL be explicitly defined.

Implicit policies SHALL NOT exist.

Deterministic

Equivalent inputs SHALL produce equivalent policy decisions unless explicitly declared adaptive.

Technology Independent

Policies SHALL remain independent of implementation technologies.

Traceable

Policy evaluations SHALL remain reconstructable.

Explainable

Every policy decision SHALL be explainable.

Governed

Policies SHALL themselves be governed Canonical Atlas Objects.

Versioned

Every policy SHALL support controlled version evolution.

Composable

Policies MAY be combined without altering their individual semantics.

8. Canonical Policy Families

Atlas defines the following canonical policy families.

Policy Family	Purpose
Governance Policy	Ownership, stewardship, approvals
Security Policy	Authentication, authorization, classification
Validation Policy	Validation rules and quality gates
Lifecycle Policy	State transitions and approvals
Workflow Policy	Workflow execution rules
Decision Policy	Decision authority and approvals
Communication Policy	Message exchange rules
Trust Policy	Trust evaluation and assurance
Compliance Policy	Regulatory and organizational obligations
Integration Policy	External system interaction

Future policy families SHALL extend this hierarchy.

